## Why This Is Asked

Assesses practical multi-cloud Terraform design, policy-as-code, drift handling, and test automation at scale—core for Oracle, Tesla, and LinkedIn-like environments.

## Key Concepts

- Provider aliasing for multi-region/multi-cloud setups
- Wrapper modules to standardize tagging and policy constraints
- Terraform Cloud/Sentinel or OPA for governance
- Drift detection workflows and isolated drift environments
- End-to-end testing with Terratest or kitchen-terraform

## Code Example

```hcl
# Wrapper module injecting standard tags
variable "environment" { type = string }
variable "cost_center" { type = string }
variable "tags" { type = map(string) }

module "service" {
  source = "../aws-azure-base"
  providers = {
    aws   = aws.us_east_1
    aws_eu = aws.eu_west_1
    azurerm = azurerm.westus2
  }
  tags = merge(var.tags, {
    Environment = var.environment
    CostCenter  = var.cost_center
  })
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you structure a Sentinel policy to enforce tag requirements and deny secrets in state?
- How would you simulate drift in tests and verify policy compliance across clouds?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of per-env isolation, simple gating, and multi-provider setups for real-world tools like Databricks and Snowflake in a beginner-friendly way.

## Key Concepts
- Env tagging across modules
- Per-env remote state backends
- Provider aliases for multi-environment isolation
- Lightweight CI gates for plan validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// backend snippet (per-env)
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "tf-state-bucket"
    key    = "env/dev/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
    dynamodb_table = "tf-locks-dev"
  }
}

// wrapper module outline
module "env_wrapper" {
  source = "./modules/env-wrapper"
  environment = var.environment
  tags = { Environment = var.environment }
}
```

